art_direction.glass_treatment:
**Decision first: the glass is a single floating pill, and it is the shop counter.** Not a full-bleed bar. Not glass cards. Not a glass PDP panel. The research is unambiguous that the dominant performance term is blurred area × dpr², and that `position:fixed` + `backdrop-filter` makes WebKit recompute the blur on *every scroll frame* — so a 560px pill instead of a 100vw bar cuts the cost 60–75%, and it is also the better-looking, more premium object. The teardown found **zero** streetwear brands blurring their nav, and Palace ships a floating pill that is opaque black. We are going to be the one that blurs — so we have to earn it, and the way we earn it is by being small, by having a contrast floor, and by knowing what we are over.

**Geometry.** Desktop: centred, `top: 20px`, max-width 560px, height 52px, `border-radius: 999px`, `pointer-events:none` on the wrapper with `pointer-events:auto` on the pill so the page behind stays clickable (Palace's exact trick). **Mobile: the pill is bottom-anchored** — `bottom: calc(14px + env(safe-area-inset-bottom))`, inset 14px each side, height 54px. That is inside the thumb arc, which is the entire conversion argument, and it means the pill is over scrolling *content* rather than over the hero video for most of the session.

**Contents — it earns its place or it is not in there.**
- Desktop: `[OT] · SHOP · DROP 001 02:14:09 · THE LIST · BAG (2)` — five items maximum, everything else behind the SHOP flyout.
- Mobile: `[OT] · SHOP · 02:14:09 · BAG (2)`.
- **On a PDP the pill morphs**: the nav items slide out and the pill becomes `[S][M][L][XL] — ADD · £48`, full-width-minus-gutters, still glass, still bottom-anchored. The persistent bottom bar on mobile *is* add-to-bag, which is the only version of that pattern that isn't tell #36. The morph is a shared-element width/content transition, 320ms, not a fade.
- Bag count is a bone dot with tabular digits. `aria-live="polite"` announces "2 items in bag".

**THE LADDER — four tiers, and Tier 2 is the product.**

**Tier 3 — universal floor, no `backdrop-filter` at all.**
```css
.ot-pill{
  background: rgb(15 15 15 / .93);
  border: 1px solid var(--ot-hairline-strong);
  box-shadow: 0 8px 32px -8px rgb(0 0 0 / .5);
}
```

**Tier 2 — THE DESIGN. Safari, Firefox, every phone. This is what we art-direct against, on an iPhone, until it looks finished.**
```css
@supports ((backdrop-filter: blur(1px)) or (-webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(1px))) {
  .ot-pill{
    background:
      linear-gradient(180deg, rgb(250 246 246 / .10), rgb(250 246 246 / .03) 42%, rgb(250 246 246 / .07)),
      var(--ot-scrim);                       /* rgb(15 15 15 / .78) — the CONTRAST FLOOR */
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(16px) saturate(175%);
            backdrop-filter: blur(16px) saturate(175%);
    box-shadow:
      inset 0  1px 0 0 var(--ot-rim),
      inset 0 -1px 0 0 rgb(250 246 246 / .10),
      0 8px 32px -8px rgb(0 0 0 / .45);
    contain: paint;
  }
}
```
`saturate(175%)` is doing more work than the blur — glass concentrates colour, and without it you get fog. Blur is capped at **16px**: above ~20px low-end devices degrade sharply for no visible gain.

The `--ot-scrim: rgb(15 15 15 / .78)` floor is non-negotiable and is the reason we can blur where nobody else does. At 0.78 over near-black, bone type clears 4.5:1 **regardless of what is behind the pill** — a bone product shot, a white-tee section, anything. It is measured once against both extremes and then locked as a token. This matters doubly because **`prefers-reduced-transparency` is not supported in Safari at all** (WebKit bug 175497) — the one media query that exists to turn glass off is unavailable on the platform whose users are most likely to have Reduce Transparency on. We cannot honour that preference on iOS, so the glass must be legible *without* it. Apple's own remedy for Liquid Glass legibility complaints was to raise nav-bar opacity; we follow them, up front.

**Tier 2.5 — adaptive tinting. This is the bespoke detail, and it costs nothing unsupported.** The pill knows what it is over. Sections carry `data-nav-theme="dark|light|media"`; one `IntersectionObserver` with `rootMargin` pinned to the pill's band flips the whole token set:
```css
.ot-pill[data-over="dark"]  { --ot-ink:#FAF6F6; --ot-scrim:rgb(15 15 15 / .78); --ot-rim:rgb(250 246 246 / .38); }
.ot-pill[data-over="light"] { --ot-ink:#0F0F0F; --ot-scrim:rgb(223 221 222 / .80); --ot-rim:rgb(255 255 255 / .85); }
```
So over the `#DFDDDE` Fit & Care section the pill inverts to smoked bone with dark type. Represent does this with `data-style` and no blur at all; we do it *with* blur, which is strictly better and is the honest answer to "adaptive light/dark tinting" without a single unsupported property.

**Tier 2.5 also solves the video collision.** The brief wants AI video backdrops *and* a glass scroll bar — blurring over playing video means re-rasterising every frame on the platform with the worst caching. The same observer that flips the theme sets `data-over="media"`, which **cross-fades to an opaque pill via `opacity` on two pre-rendered layers** (never by animating the blur radius — a transitioning blur cannot be cached):
```css
.ot-pill__glass{opacity:1;transition:opacity 240ms linear}
.ot-pill__solid{opacity:0;transition:opacity 240ms linear}
.ot-pill[data-over="media"] .ot-pill__glass{opacity:0}
.ot-pill[data-over="media"] .ot-pill__solid{opacity:1}
```
One mechanism, three payoffs: legibility, performance, and a nav that visibly responds to the page. Users read it as intention.

**Tier 1 — refraction. Chromium desktop only, and it is a garnish.** `backdrop-filter: url(#ot-refract)` is Chromium-only as of Sept 2026: WebKit bug 245510 is still NEW with PR #68614 open and `merging-blocked`; Firefox bug 1961378 is NEW and its WebRender path silently drops the filter. **`@supports` returns a false positive in Firefox**, so it cannot be the gate — we engine-sniff, reluctantly, and gate additionally on `(min-width:900px) and (pointer:fine)`. The displacement map is a rounded-rect SDF → bevel → normal map baked to a data-URI PNG (kube.io's maths), with `filterUnits="userSpaceOnUse"`, `color-interpolation-filters="sRGB"` (not optional — the SVG default is linearRGB and everything comes out washed), both `href` and `xlink:href` on the `feImage`, and a regenerate-on-resize hook because the filter region does not track the element.
**Chromatic dispersion: omitted entirely.** RGB fringing reads as glitch; OUR TIME is washed, muted, vintage-athletic. It is off-brand and it triples the displacement cost.

**If Tier 1 never ships in WebKit, nothing is missing.** That is the test the design has to pass, and it does, because we art-direct Tier 2 first and add Tier 1 last.

**Where else glass is allowed — a closed list.**
1. The cart drawer scrim (`blur(20px)` over `rgb(15 15 15 / .55)`) — blur on an *overlay*, which is where the category actually uses it.
2. The sticky size/colour bar on the collection page (`blur(10px)`), the one nav-adjacent blur found anywhere in the teardown (Sporty & Rich).
**Nowhere else. Never on a card.** A grid of `backdrop-blur` cards is tell #23 and it is the fastest way to look generated.

**Forced overrides, all tiers:**
```css
@media (prefers-reduced-transparency: reduce), (prefers-contrast: more){
  .ot-pill{backdrop-filter:none!important;-webkit-backdrop-filter:none!important;
           background:rgb(15 15 15 / .97)!important;border-color:rgb(250 246 246 / .45)!important}
}
@media (forced-colors: active){
  .ot-pill{backdrop-filter:none;background:Canvas;color:CanvasText;border:1px solid CanvasText}
}
```
`prefers-contrast: more` (Chrome 96+, FF 101+, **Safari 14.1+**) is the only working lever on Apple platforms, so it is wired alongside the unsupported one. Focus rings sit above the glass, solid `--ot-live` 2px with a 2px bone offset, never translucent.

**Performance budget, enforced:** one glass surface per viewport. `will-change: backdrop-filter` on the pill and **nothing else**. `contain: paint`. Verified in Chrome DevTools → Rendering → Paint flashing: if the pill flashes green on every scroll tick, the blur is not cached and the build does not ship.
